import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={quantumTunneling:{theory:{concept:"[[Quantum tunneling is the passage of a particle through a barrier even though its energy is lower than the height of the barrier \u2014 something classical physics forbids.]] A ball without enough energy always rolls back from a hill. A particle such as an electron, however, behaves like a wave: inside the barrier its wave does not stop at once but fades gradually, and if the barrier is thin enough, a small part of the wave comes out on the other side. The transmission probability T falls very quickly as the barrier gets wider or higher and as the particle gets heavier."},section:{wall:{title:"The classical picture: a wall",text:"A barrier is a region where the electron would need more potential energy V\u2080 than the energy E it has \u2014 like a hill too high for a rolling ball. Classically the answer is simple: if E < V\u2080 the ball always bounces back, if E > V\u2080 it always gets over. The ring in the simulation is such a classical ball with the same energy."},wave:{title:"The electron is a wave",text:"[[Every particle has a de Broglie wavelength \u03BB = h / p.]] For an electron of a few electronvolts it is about 1 nm \u2014 as large as a few atoms, so in barriers of that size its wave nature cannot be ignored. The simulation shows a wave packet: the filled curve is the probability density |\u03C8|\xB2, which tells where the electron is likely to be found."},inside:{title:"What happens inside the barrier",text:"Inside a barrier that is too high (E < V\u2080) the wave does not oscillate any more: it falls off like e^(\u2212\u03BAx), with \u03BA = \u221A(2m(V\u2080 \u2212 E)) / \u0127. Over the penetration depth 1/\u03BA it gets e \u2248 2.7 times weaker. If the barrier is only a few penetration depths thick, the wave is still not zero at its far side, and it continues there as a smaller wave with the same wavelength."},controls:{title:"What decides the chance to pass",text:"[[For a thick barrier T \u2248 e^(\u22122\u03BAa).]] The width a is in the exponent: doubling the width roughly squares an already small T (1 % becomes 0.01 %). A higher barrier or a slower electron makes \u03BA larger. The mass is inside \u03BA as well: a proton is 1836 times heavier than an electron, so it tunnels through the same barrier with a chance smaller by many orders of magnitude. That is why we do not see balls or people tunnel through walls."},chance:{title:"A chance, not half an electron",text:"The wave splits into a reflected and a passed part, but the electron does not split. A detector finds the whole electron either on the left or on the right \u2014 at random, with the probabilities R and T, and T + R = 1. Send a million electrons with T = 10 %, and about 100 000 of them get through. The electron has the same energy on both sides: it does not lose energy in the barrier."},above:{title:"Reflection above the barrier",text:"Even when E > V\u2080 the electron is not certain to pass: waves are partly reflected wherever the medium changes suddenly, like light at a window. At special energies the reflections from the two edges cancel each other and T = 1 exactly: this happens when a whole number of half-wavelengths fits inside the barrier (k\u2032a = \u03C0, 2\u03C0, \u2026). A classical ball knows nothing of this."},history:{title:"From radioactivity to a Nobel Prize",text:"In 1927 Friedrich Hund first used tunneling to describe molecules. In 1928 George Gamow, and independently Ronald Gurney and Edward Condon, explained alpha decay with it: an alpha particle escapes from a nucleus through a barrier it could never climb, and the tunneling chance decides whether a nucleus lives microseconds or billions of years. Leo Esaki (tunnel diode, Nobel Prize 1973) and Gerd Binnig and Heinrich Rohrer (scanning tunneling microscope, Nobel Prize 1986) turned it into technology. The 2025 Nobel Prize in Physics (John Clarke, Michel Devoret, John Martinis) was given for tunneling of a whole electric circuit."},today:{title:"Tunneling around us",text:"The Sun shines thanks to tunneling: protons in its core are too slow to overcome their electric repulsion and fuse only because they tunnel. Flash memory in phones and USB sticks stores data by pushing electrons through a thin insulating layer. A scanning tunneling microscope moves a sharp tip a fraction of a nanometre above a surface; the tunneling current changes about ten times for every 0.1 nm, so it can see single atoms."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"E < V\u2080: can the electron be found beyond the barrier? (Yes, with the chance T.) What happens to T if you make the barrier twice as thick? (It becomes much smaller \u2014 roughly squared for thick barriers.) Will a proton with the same energy tunnel more easily? (No \u2014 far less, because it is heavier.) E > V\u2080: does everything pass? (Not always \u2014 some of the wave reflects, except at resonance.) Is the electron slower after tunneling? (No \u2014 it has the same energy and speed.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{deBroglie:"de Broglie wavelength of a particle with momentum p",sum:"Every electron is either reflected or passes",kappa:"How fast the wave dies out inside the barrier",approx:"Thick barrier: T falls exponentially with the width",exact:"Exact chance to pass for E < V\u2080",above:"Exact chance to pass for E > V\u2080 (T = 1 when k\u2032a = n\u03C0)",schrodinger:"The Schr\xF6dinger equation, solved step by step in the simulation"},variable:{kappa:"Decay constant inside the barrier, 1/nm",mass:"Mass of the particle (electron: 9.11\xB710\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",hbar:"Reduced Planck constant \u0127 = h / 2\u03C0 = 1.055\xB710\u207B\xB3\u2074 J\xB7s",density:"Probability density: where the electron is likely to be found"},assumption:{oneDimension:"The electron moves along one line. It is free and non-relativistic, and no forces act on it except the barrier.",rectangle:"The barrier is a rectangle with sharp edges. Real barriers (the gap under a microscope tip, the barrier of a nucleus) have smooth shapes.",packet:"T is the result for a wave of exactly one energy. The wave packet contains a small spread of energies (smaller for a longer packet), so the share that passes differs slightly from T.",numeric:"The wave packet is computed numerically (Crank\u2013Nicolson method) on a grid with a step of 0.015 nm in a 60 nm box; the wave never reaches the ends of the box.",drawing:"The curve shows the probability density |\u03C8|\xB2, drawn on the line of the energy E \u2014 a common drawing convention: its height is not an energy. The barrier is drawn at least 3 pixels wide, and the passed part can be shown magnified ten times (dashed). Not to scale.",slow:"The motion is slowed down enormously: one second of animation is about 4 femtoseconds (4\xB710\u207B\xB9\u2075 s)."},param:{energy:"Energy of the electron",barrierHeight:"Height of the barrier",barrierWidth:"Width of the barrier",packetWidth:"Length of the wave packet",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{energy:"Kinetic energy of the electron. 1 eV is the energy an electron gains when it passes a voltage of 1 V.",barrierHeight:"The potential energy the electron would need to get over the barrier. 0 means no barrier.",barrierWidth:"The thickness of the barrier. Neighbouring atoms in a solid are about 0.1\u20130.3 nm apart.",packetWidth:"Spread \u03C3 of the packet's probability density. A longer packet has a more exactly defined energy."},preset:{slow:"1 eV",medium:"3 eV",fast:"6 eV",noBarrier:"No barrier",typical:"5 eV",high:"10 eV",thin:"0.1 nm (one atom)",stm:"0.5 nm (microscope gap)",thick:"1 nm"},measure:{transmission:"Chance to pass (theory)",transmitted:"Passed (wave packet)",reflected:"Reflected (wave packet)",classical:"Classical ball passes",reflection:"Chance to be reflected (theory)",wavelength:"de Broglie wavelength",speed:"Speed of the electron",decayLength:"Penetration depth (0 when E \u2265 V\u2080)",inside:"Inside the barrier",total:"Total probability",physicalTime:"Real time",log10:"Decimal logarithm of T",log10Proton:"log\u2081\u2080 T for a proton with the same energy"},graph:{probabilities:"Where the electron is found vs time",inside:"Probability inside the barrier"},view:{classicalBall:"classical ball",barrier:"barrier: V\u2080 = {height}, a = {width}",noBarrier:"no barrier",reflected:"reflected: {value}",transmitted:"passed: {value}",zoom:"passed part \xD7{factor}",axis:"x",scale:"5 nm",legend:"filled: probability density |\u03C8|\xB2 \xB7 dashed: magnified \xB7 ring: classical ball",finished:"Passed: {passed} (theory for one exact energy: {theory})",below:"E < V\u2080: a classical ball bounces back \xB7 quantum chance to pass: {theory}",above:"E > V\u2080: a classical ball gets over \xB7 quantum chance to pass: {theory}",classicalOption:"Classical ball",zoomOption:"Magnify the passed part",realPartOption:"Real part of \u03C8 (the wave)"},challenge:{wall:{title:"Through the wall",description:"Build a barrier that a classical ball cannot cross (E < V\u2080), but that the electron passes with a chance of at least 20 %.",hint:"Make the barrier thin and its top only a little above E."},closed:{title:"Almost closed",description:"With a barrier no wider than 0.5 nm, make the chance to pass smaller than 0.1 %.",hint:"A higher barrier (a larger V\u2080 \u2212 E) makes the wave die out faster inside it."},bounce:{title:"Turned back by a low barrier",description:"Give the electron more energy than the barrier (E > V\u2080), yet make it reflect with a chance of at least 10 %.",hint:"A classical ball would always pass. Waves also reflect at a sudden step: try E only a little above V\u2080."},see:{title:"Watch it pass",description:"Run the experiment with E < V\u2080 so that at least 30 % of the wave packet ends up beyond the barrier.",hint:"A thin barrier with its top close to E. Press start and wait until the run ends."},onePercent:{title:"One in a hundred",description:"With E < V\u2080, set the chance to pass to 1 % (\xB1 0.1 %).",hint:"Change the width or the energy in small steps: every extra 0.1 nm of width makes T several times smaller."},perfect:{title:"An invisible barrier",description:"With a barrier of at least 3 eV and E > V\u2080, make the electron pass with a chance of at least 99.9 %.",hint:"Resonance: T = 1 when a whole number of half-wavelengths fits inside the barrier (k\u2032a = \u03C0). For V\u2080 = 5 eV and a = 0.5 nm, try E near 6.5 eV."},proton:{title:"Can a proton tunnel?",description:"Find a setup with E < V\u2080 in which a proton with the same energy would pass with a chance of at least 10\u207B\u2076 (log\u2081\u2080 T_p \u2265 \u22126).",hint:"A proton is 1836 times heavier, so its \u03BA is 43 times larger. Use the thinnest barrier and make V\u2080 \u2212 E tiny."}}}},i={sim:a};export{i as default,a as sim};
